1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,034
How do you weigh water you can barely see? This gram of powder looks dry.

2
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:07,718
Let's measure its water content by counting electrons.

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:13,447
Weigh the sample, then seal the vial with a crimp cap. For this powder,

4
00:00:13,447 --> 00:00:19,214
the oven is set to one hundred and sixty degrees Celsius. A needle pierces the cap.

5
00:00:19,214 --> 00:00:22,819
Dry argon carries the released water into the titration cell.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,683
Inside the cell, two electrodes have different jobs. The generator makes iodine.

7
00:00:28,683 --> 00:00:33,003
The indicator tells us when a little iodine finally remains.

8
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:39,910
At the generator anode, two iodide ions give up two electrons to form iodine.

9
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:44,790
At the cathode, protonated base can accept electrons and release hydrogen.

10
00:00:44,790 --> 00:00:48,782
The coulometer records the charge passing through this circuit.

11
00:00:49,467 --> 00:00:56,316
Now look inside the reagent. Methanol combines with sulfur dioxide in the presence of a base

12
00:00:56,316 --> 00:01:02,309
to form methyl sulfite. An abundance of methanol favors this side of the equilibrium.

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,599
As water arrives, iodine oxidizes methyl sulfite to methyl sulfate.

14
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:12,658
The base accepts the released protons, and iodine returns to iodide.

15
00:01:12,658 --> 00:01:19,096
Follow the two highlighted molecules. One mole of iodine consumes one mole of water.

16
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:24,836
Put the preparation and oxidation steps together. Methanol, sulfur dioxide,

17
00:01:24,836 --> 00:01:29,872
base, iodine, and water give methyl sulfate and iodide salts.

18
00:01:29,872 --> 00:01:34,908
Every water molecule counted costs two electrons at the generator.

19
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:41,666
How does the instrument know when to stop? A small, fixed polarizing current passes between

20
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:47,732
two platinum pins. While the water consumes the iodine, a relatively high voltage is needed

21
00:01:47,732 --> 00:01:49,349
to maintain that current.

22
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:56,300
Once iodine persists, one pin reduces it to iodide while the other oxidizes iodide.

23
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:02,119
Charge moves more easily. The voltage drops and stays low. That's the endpoint.

24
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:08,175
Watch the generator current over time. It rises as the sample releases water,

25
00:02:08,175 --> 00:02:14,377
then falls toward the background. The shaded area above the drift baseline gives the charge

26
00:02:14,377 --> 00:02:18,512
attributable to our sample, after any separate vial blank correction.

27
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:23,866
For a constant current, charge is current times time. When current varies,

28
00:02:23,866 --> 00:02:28,921
add up all the little areas. That's the integral of current over time.

29
00:02:28,921 --> 00:02:34,753
Divide the corrected charge by twice the Faraday constant, then multiply by the molar mass

30
00:02:34,753 --> 00:02:35,531
of water.

31
00:02:36,233 --> 00:02:41,138
Here, two point one four two coulombs give two hundred micrograms of water.

32
00:02:41,138 --> 00:02:46,043
Our sample weighed one gram. That's two hundred parts per million by mass.

33
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:53,505
A result depends on the reagent as well as the electronics. Ketones can form water in methanol

34
00:02:53,505 --> 00:03:00,277
and bias a result high. An unsuitable reagent pH or too little methanol can disrupt the reaction.

35
00:03:00,277 --> 00:03:06,253
A dry gas path and a measured blank help separate sample water from background moisture.

36
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:13,653
Try the Karl Fischer cell yourself in Adam's Laboratory. Bergslagen Chemical Systems Corporation.
